        'Three-point perspective',
        'A worm\'s-eye view',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'The horizon line is the projection of the viewer\'s infinite distance, so it always sits at eye height in the scene. Once that single fact is placed, every object\'s behaviour — tops below the horizon, undersides above — follows mechanically. This is the one rule that untangles every construction.',
    },
    {
      q: 'A 1.8 m person stands 4 m away; an identical person stands 12 m away. How tall does the distant one appear relative to the near one?',
      choices: [
        'Same height — people stay the same size',
        'Twice as small — distance subtracts size evenly',
        'A third the height — apparent size falls as 1/distance (12 m is three times 4 m)',
        'Nine times smaller',
      ],
      answer: 2,
      why: 'Apparent size scales with the inverse of distance: triple the distance, one-third the image height. Perspective is that arithmetic made exact, and the vanishing point is simply where the inverse law drives every receding parallel line.',
    },
    {
      q: 'In correct perspective, the ellipse at the top of a mug (a circle seen at an angle) must have its minor axis:',
      choices: [
        'Horizontal',
        'Pointing toward the vanishing point of the circle\'s plane — misaligned ellipses are the most common perspective tell in amateur drawing',
        'Perfectly circular — circles stay circles',
        'Twice as wide as tall',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'A circle in perspective becomes an ellipse, and its short (minor) axis aligns with the direction of recession. Get the axis right and the mug, plate, wheel or tree-trunk reads as solid; get it wrong — the all-too-common sideways ellipse — and no amount of shading repairs the surface.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Chinese handscroll landscapes and isometric game art both decline linear perspective. Why is that a legitimate choice, not an error?',
      choices: [
        'They could not do the maths',
        'Perspective is one projection system among several, each with assumptions — the scroll moves the viewpoint along the journey, isometric keeps parallels parallel and measurements comparable',
        'Neither culture had windows',
        'The subjects were too far away',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'Panofsky called perspective a "symbolic form": it encodes one fixed viewer in one moment. A Song handscroll unfolds time instead (the eye travels, the viewpoint moves); an isometric drawing trades depth drama for measurable truth. Choose the projection to fit the question — that is the mastery the convention makes possible.',
    },
  ],
};

