---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/visual-arts/intermediate/perspective-and-depth">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate Visual Arts lesson. It builds on the foundational
    <strong>value, colour and composition</strong> lesson — tone and arrangement carry most
    of a picture's realism — and on the sibling intermediate lesson <strong>line, shape and
    value</strong>. No geometry beyond ruler-and-pencil straight lines is needed.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>name the pre-geometric depth cues and deploy them before any vanishing point is drawn;</li>
    <li>explain what Brunelleschi demonstrated and what Alberti codified — the picture plane as a window;</li>
    <li>set the horizon at eye level and choose one-, two- or three-point construction to match the subject;</li>
    <li>draw ellipses whose minor axes point along the direction of recession;</li>
    <li>say why perspective is one projection system among several, and when to decline it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Perspective is <strong>the mathematics of the window</strong> — a geometric system for depth
    that conquered Western art in one generation. Learning it is learning a convention so
    powerful it feels like vision itself; mastering it means knowing when to use it and when to
    decline. The arc of this page runs deliberately from the cues that need no geometry
    (they are how every child's drawing and every medieval fresco already does depth) to the
    fifteenth-century invention that turned those instincts into a calculation, to the modern
    artists who learned the system precisely in order to dismantle it. Depth on a flat surface
    is always an argument; perspective is the most persuasive one ever built — and the most
    instructive to see through.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the pre-geometric cues, the mirror trick, the constructions, the convention" widget="equationStep" config={perspCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> step through the four panels in order and watch one idea
    arrive twice — first as instincts (overlap, size, atmosphere, position) that need no
    geometry at all, then as the mirror trick that turns those instincts into a calculation.
    Panel 3 is the working toolkit: notice that the one fixed rule in every construction is
    the horizon sitting at eye level. Panel 4 then declines the rule book on purpose —
    scroll, isometric drawing, lens — a system used knowingly rather than merely obeyed.
  </p>

  <h2>Depth without geometry: the cues that come first</h2>
  <p>
    Linear perspective is only two centuries old as a <em>system</em>, but flat pictures have
    carried depth for millennia on five cues the visual system reads natively.
    <strong>Overlap</strong>: an object cutting another off is in front — the strongest,
    earliest cue (without it, no crowd picture works). <strong>Size</strong>: of two similar
    objects, the larger reads nearer. <strong>Position</strong>: in the Western picture
    convention, higher on the surface reads farther (the ground-plane habit of the eye).
    <strong>Atmospheric perspective</strong>: air scatters blue light, so distance arrives
    lighter, bluer, less contrasted — a physics lesson painted into every landscape.
    <strong>Texture gradient</strong>: surfaces get finer and denser with distance — the
    pebbles shrink, the grass compresses (Gibson made this cue the foundation of ecological
    optics). Add <strong>shading</strong> (the value lesson's modelling) and you have the
    painter's complete pre-geometric kit. Medieval painting used it masterfully: Giotto's
    figures overlap and shade convincingly while their architecture refuses the window —
    proof that the cues and the geometry are separable skills, and that most of depth's
    persuasiveness never needed a vanishing point.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="five cues that need no vanishing point" widget="match" config={perspCueMatch} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> most of a picture's depth comes from cues — overlap, size,
    position, atmosphere, texture — that never needed a vanishing point at all. The
    Renaissance added the system that makes those instincts exact.
  </p>

  <h2>Brunelleschi's mirror and Alberti's window (c. 1415–1435)</h2>
  <p>
    The system arrived as a public demonstration. Brunelleschi — the architect of the
    Florence dome — painted the Baptistry of San Giovanni on a small panel, drilled a
    peephole at its vanishing point, and set up the view facing the real building: a
    viewer looking through the hole saw the painting, or, raising a mirror, saw the
    painting reflected against the Baptistry itself — and could not tell them apart.
    (The account comes from his biographer Manetti; the device used a mirror and a
    peephole to force the single, fixed viewpoint that perspective assumes.) Within a
    generation Alberti codified it in <em>De pictura</em> (1435): the picture is a
    <strong>window</strong>, an intersection of sight-lines from a fixed eye to the
    scene — the <em>costruzione legittima</em>, with its grid of receding lines and its
    measuring method. The fit with Florence was perfect: architects surveying,
    cartographers mapping, merchants pricing — a city that wanted the world calculable
    got a picture system to match. Masaccio's Trinity (c. 1427) put the new maths in a
    church; within decades every trained painter in Europe was converging lines.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="the mirror trick and its codification, in order" widget="order" config={perspDemoOrder} />

  <h2>The constructions: horizon, points, and what each is for</h2>
  <p>
    The whole apparatus rests on one fact: <strong>the horizon line is always the
    viewer's eye level</strong> — the projection of infinitely distant sight-lines.
    Set the horizon first and everything follows: we look <em>down</em> on every surface
    below it (floors, table tops — their receding lines converge upward toward the
    horizon) and <em>up</em> at everything above it (ceilings, undersides — converging
    down). Then choose the system by the geometry of the subject.
    <strong>One-point perspective</strong>: one face of the subject parallel to the
    picture plane, all depth lines converging to a single point — hallways, roads, and
    the classic classroom construction: draw the front rectangle, mark the vanishing
    point on the horizon, project the corners, close the box at the depth you choose.
    <strong>Two-point perspective</strong> — the standard for standing objects: the
    object turned so only its verticals stay parallel to the picture plane; each set of
    horizontal edges converges to its own vanishing point on the horizon, left and right
    (the corner of a building is the canonical subject — place the near vertical edge,
    project to both points, place the second vertical at estimated depth, project back).
    <strong>Three-point</strong>: the camera looks up or down, verticals converge too —
    the worm's-eye and bird's-eye drama of film posters and comics, best rationed.
    Apparent size obeys the inverse law throughout: an object twice as far appears half
    as tall (worked example: two identical 1.8 m figures at 4 m and 12 m — the distant
    one draws one-third the height). And circles become <strong>ellipses</strong>, with
    the single most violated rule in drawing: the ellipse's minor axis must point along
    the direction of recession — toward that plane's vanishing point. The sideways
    ellipse on a mug, plate or wheel is the amateur tell; fixing it alone upgrades a
    drawing.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="one-, two- or three-point? file the jobs" widget="sort-buckets" config={perspSysSort} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the horizon line is where the sky meets the ground, so a
  street scene or an interior has no horizon." The horizon of a drawing is not the Earth's
  edge — it is the viewer's eye level, projected through the scene. Indoors, windowless, at
  midnight, it still exists: draw it at the height of your eyes, and every tabletop recedes
  up toward it while every ceiling edge recedes down toward it. Landscape or hallway, the
  first construction line is the same.</p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="one belief in this horizon briefing is wrong" widget="spot-error" config={perspHorSpot} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the horizon is set by the eye, not by the scene, and the choice
    of one-, two- or three-point construction follows from the subject's geometry. Those
    constructions build imagined scenes; drawing from life needs calibration tools instead.
  </p>

  <h2>Measuring what you see</h2>
  <p>
    Perspective constructions are for imagined scenes; observation needs the atelier's
    calibration tools. <strong>Pencil-and-thumb measurement</strong>: at arm's length,
    lock your elbow, use the pencil to compare any width in the scene against any
    height — comparative, never absolute (arm length varies; ratios transfer).
    <strong>Sight-size</strong>: stand back, place subject and paper so they appear the
    same size from one fixed spot, and step back repeatedly to compare — the method's
    rigidity is its feature, because a fixed viewpoint makes perspective automatic.
    <strong>Plumb-line and angle-checking</strong>: verticals against a dropped line,
    angles against clock positions. The unifying habit is the fixed viewpoint: every
    perspective error in observational drawing is really a viewpoint error — the head
    drifted between marks, the eye moved, and the window quietly changed. (This is why
    the observation lesson next door treats "keep your seat" as technique.)
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="pencil-and-thumb vs sight-size" widget="compare" config={perspMeasCompare} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "accurate drawing is a matter of a steady hand and a good
  eye." The atelier's tools say otherwise: almost every perspective error in observational
  drawing is a viewpoint error — the head drifted between marks, and the window quietly
  changed. Lock the elbow, fix the seat, return to the same spot, and even a shaky hand
  outdraws a confident one standing anywhere.</p>

  <h2>One system among several</h2>
  <p>
    Perspective's power makes it easy to mistake for perception itself — it is one
    projection among many, each encoding different assumptions. <strong>Chinese
    handscroll landscape</strong> (Song dynasty masterworks) uses parallel, oblique
    conventions and, more radically, a <em>moving</em> viewpoint: the scroll unrolls
    through time, the eye travels the journey, and no single vanishing point exists —
    a cinematic logic centuries before cinema. <strong>Isometric and axonometric
    projection</strong> (the architect's and the game-designer's drawing) keep all
    parallels parallel: no convergence, no size falloff — depth is legible and
    distances measurable, which is exactly why engineers declined the window.
    <strong>Camera lenses</strong> add their own corrections and distortions (wide-angle
    stretch, telephoto compression) — and the deep subtlety: perspective look is
    determined by <em>viewpoint</em>, not focal length; the lens forces you to stand
    where you stand, which is why zooming is not the same as walking. Panofsky's
    formulation is the adult position: perspective is a <strong>symbolic form</strong> —
    a historically specific decision to encode one viewer, one eye, one moment. It is
    not truth; it is a remarkably persuasive agreement.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="three jobs, three projection systems" widget="branch" config={perspProjBranch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "correct perspective" equals realistic drawing.
  Most perceptual realism comes from the pre-geometric cues — value, edges, texture,
  atmosphere — while strict linear perspective is one layer, and applying it rigidly to
  every subject often looks <em>staged</em>: real vision scans, binocular vision
  disagrees slightly with itself, and cameras of the eye have none of the window's
  rigidity. Drawings that feel alive (Degas's cropped, high-viewpoint racecourses;
  Hokusai's flattened waves) are usually perspective-rule-breakers with cue-mastery.</p>

  <h2>Shadows, occlusion and the anamorphic trick</h2>
  <p>
    Two depth phenomena deserve their own attention. First, <strong>cast shadows</strong>:
    a shadow is depth information twice over — it anchors an object to a surface (a
    figure without a contact shadow floats like a sticker) and it encodes the light's
    direction, which the perspective lesson's constructions rarely handle. The drawing
    rules are few and reliable: shadows fall on the surface, obey that surface's
    geometry (a shadow across a step bends with the step), are darkest at contact and
    softest at distance, and read most truthful when drawn lighter than instinct
    suggests — beginners almost always over-darken them. Second, <strong>anamorphosis</strong>:
    perspective run deliberately wrong. Holbein's <em>The Ambassadors</em> (1533) hides
    a skull stretched across the lower foreground that snaps into legibility only from
    one glancing viewpoint — a painted proof that the system's geometry is exact enough
    to be broken precisely: the distortion is computed, not casual. Trompe-l'œil
    ceiling frescoes (the Baroque's Heaven opened over the nave, the previous
    lesson's Bernini at architectural scale) are anamorphosis made architecture.
    Both phenomena repay study because they demonstrate the system's edges:
    shadows show where pictorial light logic lives outside linear construction;
    anamorphosis shows that a viewer's single point of view is the system's
    hidden foundation — the assumption no Renaissance picture could state,
    and every modern one could break.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="true or false — shadows and the anamorphic trick" widget="judge" config={perspShadowJudge} />

  <p>
    One more depth channel belongs beside the geometry: <strong>focus and blur</strong>.
    The eye only resolves sharply at the centre of gaze; everything else is soft — and
    cameras imitate this with depth of field, so blurred regions read as "not where
    attention lives" and, in landscape layers, as far away. Painters knew the effect
    centuries before lenses: Leonardo's sfumato softens distant mountains into air,
    Vermeer's backgrounds dissolve into soft blobs while the pearl stays sharp, and the
    Impressionists' broken edges do double duty as optical blur. The modern camera
    literalised it (the long lens's creamy separation is depth-of-field as
    composition), and the <strong>tilt-shift effect</strong> inverts it
    delightfully: blur a real landscape's top and bottom with sharp middle and
    the brain reads a miniature — the strongest proof that blur is a distance
    symbol, not just an accident of optics. For the drawer, the practical
    rules: sharpness is attention (spend it where the focal point lives),
    softness is atmosphere, and a drawing in which everything is equally
    sharp reads flat no matter how correct its vanishing points are.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="name the blur vocabulary" widget="cloze" config={perspBlurCloze} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>You are drawing a street scene: rooftops above your eye level, road markings below it. In which directions should each set of lines converge, and what have you drawn?</li>
    <li>Two identical 1.8 m figures stand at 4 m and at 12 m from you. How tall do you draw the distant one, and which law fixes the answer?</li>
    <li>You have drawn a mug's rim as a cleanly proportioned ellipse, yet the mug still reads wrong. What is the first thing to check?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Everything converges to the horizon, which is your eye
    level: above it you see undersides sloping down toward it, below it you see tops sloping
    up toward it — the drawing is exactly correct, not a mistake. (2) One-third the height:
    apparent size falls as the inverse of distance, and 12 m is three times 4 m — perspective
    is that arithmetic made exact. (3) The minor axis. A well-proportioned ellipse still
    reads wrong unless its short axis points along the direction of recession, toward the
    vanishing point of its surface.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Depth has pre-geometric cues — overlap, size, position, atmosphere, texture — that work with no vanishing point at all.</li>
    <li>Perspective is a convention: the picture plane treated as a window from one fixed, one-eyed viewpoint (Brunelleschi demonstrated it; Alberti codified it).</li>
    <li>The horizon line is always the viewer's eye level — the one rule that untangles every construction.</li>
    <li>One-, two- and three-point systems differ in how many edge directions converge; apparent size obeys the inverse distance law throughout.</li>
    <li>Circles become ellipses, and the minor axis must point along the recession — the most violated rule in drawing.</li>
    <li>Observational accuracy is viewpoint discipline: fixed seat, locked elbow, comparative measurement.</li>
    <li>Scrolls, isometric drawing and lenses are legitimate alternatives; knowing when to decline the window is the mastery.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling intermediate lessons complete the draughtsman's toolkit: <strong>drawing from
    observation</strong> trains the fixed-viewpoint measuring habit this page explained,
    while <strong>line, shape and value</strong> and <strong>colour theory</strong> supply
    the cue-mastery that does most of the realism.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Learn the rules, then choose: the deliberate break.</strong> The modern
    departures were made by complete masters. Cézanne tilted tabletops and shifted
    viewpoints within one still life, trading the window for structural honesty —
    "treat nature by the cylinder, the sphere, the cone" was a re-founding, not a
    relaxation. Cubism (Picasso, Braque, 1907–14) made the break systematic: multiple
    viewpoints fused in one image, the window shattered into its facets — arguably the
    first art movement whose central subject was the convention of perspective itself.
    The pedagogical sequence matters: rules dismantled from the inside read as
    authority; rules never learned read as error. Every art form in this atlas repeats
    the pattern — the perspective lesson is where drawing learns it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Perspective in the machines.</strong> Every 3-D game, film CG shot and
    architecture render runs the same maths as Alberti, generalised: a projection
    matrix intersecting sight-lines with a picture plane — the Renaissance window is
    literally a subroutine. Curvilinear (fisheye) perspective — straight lines curving,
    as they do across a wide field of real vision — has a working history (aerial
    photography, wide-format cinema, M. C. Escher's experiments) and a faithful
    mathematics (six-point systems), waiting for whoever needs vision's full
    hemisphere rather than its window. The vanishing point of verticals in four- and
    five-point systems is a nice exercise for the ambitious.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> How much depth judgement is perspective-naive?
    Evidence from cultures with receded-corner carpentry and from optic-flow research
    suggests the gradient and motion cues do heavy lifting that linear perspective
    merely formalises. Why does the "correct" Renaissance window feel <em>natural</em>
    to viewers whose eyes never hold still (the frame is a cognitive accomplishment as
    much as an optical one)? And will AR headsets — windows pinned to the world —
    produce a new projection convention, the way the Renaissance mirror once did?
    The convention has broken and reformed before; the geometry underneath has not
    changed once since 1435.
  </p>
